# Unity First Person - ComS 4370 Project 2B
# Author - Dylan Topic - Dylan004@iastate.edu
First person dungeon escape made with Unity 6 / URP for ComS 4370. You spawn 
in a walled arena with two AI agents that hunt you down. To escape you have 
to reach the exit slab, which is locked behind a 3D Tic-Tac-Toe minigame 
using the Minimax logic from Project 2A.

---

## How to run

Open the project in Unity 6 (6000.4.4f1 or newer). Open `Assets/Scenes/MainLevel.unity` 
and press the Play button.

If you want a standalone build, go to File > Build Profiles, add MainLevel to 
the Scenes In Build list, and build for Windows.

---

## Controls

- Mouse - look around
- WASD - move
- Left Shift (hold) - sprint
- Space - jump
- Left Click - select cells / press buttons in the minigame

---

## How to play

When you start, intro text flashes telling you to dodge the enemies, solve 
the puzzle, and escape. Walk around the arena and avoid the two AI agents. 
If they spot you they will chase you and deal aura damage when they get 
close. Your HP is shown in the top left.

To escape, find the cyan slab at the north end of the arena and walk into 
it. This pauses the world and opens the 3D Tic-Tac-Toe minigame. Pick a 
difficulty (Easy, Medium, or Hard) and beat the AI. The Minimax search depth 
scales with the difficulty. If you win, the slab turns green - walk back 
through it to complete the level. If you lose, the world resumes and the 
enemies are still hunting you, so heal up and try again.

If your HP hits 0, you die and get a Restart prompt.

---

## Project structure

    Project2B_FirstPerson/
     Assets/
       Scenes/
         MainLevel.unity         - the only scene, has the arena, player, AI, exit
       Scripts/
         Player/
           PlayerMovement.cs     - WASD, mouse look, sprint, jump
           PlayerHealth.cs       - HP, take damage, die, events for HUD/death
           HUD.cs                - reads PlayerHealth and updates the HP text
           DeathScreen.cs        - shows YOU DIED overlay and reloads the scene
           WinScreen.cs          - shows LEVEL COMPLETE when player walks through unlocked exit
           IntroText.cs          - shaky intro flash at scene start
         AI/
           EnemyAI.cs            - finite state machine, search/chase/aura damage
           Billboard.cs          - makes the floating name labels always face the camera
         Minigame/
           MinigameManager.cs    - opens/closes the minigame, pauses the world, picks difficulty
           ExitLock.cs           - trigger on the cyan slab, recolors it green when beaten
           TicTacToeBoardUI.cs   - builds the 4x4x4 board UI, handles clicks and AI turns
           Minimax.cs            - ported from Project 2A, alpha-beta with move ordering
           BoardState.cs         - ported from Project 2A, the 4x4x4 board model
           WinLineGenerator.cs   - ported from Project 2A, generates all 76 winning lines
           WinLine.cs            - ported from Project 2A, struct for a win line
           Player.cs             - ported from Project 2A, X/O/None enum
       Materials/                - URP materials for the player, enemies, exit slab
       Prefabs/                  - enemy prefab
     Packages/                   - AI Navigation, Input System, TMP

---

## The arena

The play area is a walled unit zone with:

- four outer walls so you cant leave the arena
- multiple interior obstacle blocks for cover and pathing
- a tilted hill block for terrain variation
- a baked NavMesh covering the walkable ground (the AI uses this to path around obstacles)
- a cyan slab at the north end - this is the exit lock

---

## The AI agents

There are two types of enemies, each running the same EnemyAI finite state machine 
with three states (Search, Chase, Attack) but configured with completely 
different stats to feel like distinct characters.

- BRUTE - deep red, larger scale, slow speed (2.5), short sight (5), wide 
  field of view (140), big aura range (4), heavy damage (12 per tick)
- STALKER - pale cyan, thinner scale, fast speed (5.5), long sight (10), 
  narrow field of view (75), small aura range (2), light damage (4 per tick 
  but ticks every 0.5s)

Both have a NavMeshAgent, a CapsuleCollider so the player physically bumps 
off them, and a floating world-space name label that always faces the 
camera. Each agent uses random NavMesh point sampling to wander while 
searching, switches to chase when it sees the player (distance + FOV cone + 
raycast line of sight check), and switches to attack inside aura range 
where it ticks damage continuously.

---

## The exit lock minigame

The cyan slab at the north wall has a Box Collider trigger. When the player 
walks into it the world pauses (Time.timeScale = 0) and a difficulty panel 
appears with Easy, Medium, and Hard buttons. After picking one, a 4x4x4 
Tic-Tac-Toe board is built procedurally on the UI canvas as four stacked 
4x4 grids of buttons.

The AI uses the Minimax logic from Project 2A with alpha-beta pruning. The 
three difficulty levels are:

- Easy - search depth 1 with 70% chance of playing a random move (lets the 
  player win most of the time)
- Medium - search depth 3 with 15% blunder chance
- Hard - search depth 5 with 0% blunder chance (close to optimal play, ~3s 
  per move on an empty board)

When the player wins the Tic-Tac-Toe game, the slab turns green and the 
lock is unlocked. Walking back through the green slab triggers the LEVEL 
COMPLETE overlay.

When the player loses the Tic-Tac-Toe game they will lose 50 hp instantly.

---

## State machines and events

The EnemyAI FSM transitions between Search, Chase, and Attack based on a 
sight check that combines:

- distance to player vs sight range
- angle between agent forward and player direction vs field of view
- a raycast from eye height to make sure no wall blocks the view

The minigame uses C# Action callbacks to pass results back. When ExitLock 
opens the minigame it passes a callback that receives the win/loss result, 
and the same pattern is used between MinigameManager and TicTacToeBoardUI.

PlayerHealth fires `OnHealthChanged` and `OnDied` events that the HUD and 
DeathScreen subscribe to. ExitLock fires `OnExitReached` when the player 
walks through after unlocking, which WinScreen subscribes to.

---

## Notes

- only one scene, no level progression
- Restart and Play Again both reload MainLevel via SceneManager
- the Minimax port from Project 2A is unchanged - the same `Minimax.cs`, 
  `BoardState.cs`, `WinLineGenerator.cs`, `WinLine.cs`, and `Player.cs` 
  files are dropped in
- the Tic-Tac-Toe board is built procedurally at runtime instead of being 
  set up in the editor, so all 64 cells exist as Unity Button components 
  with their own click listeners
- emission color changes on the exit slab go through `material.SetColor` 
  with HasProperty checks so the same code works on URP and Built-in